            {
                "entry_id": "02ed68a8-bbec-312b-8db2-8c76187a8f77",
                "word": "Lovecraftian",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "20eaaa7f-0044-44f0-9b3a-ffecd1af915e",
                        "entry_id": "02ed68a8-bbec-312b-8db2-8c76187a8f77",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Lovecraftians"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                        "entry_id": "02ed68a8-bbec-312b-8db2-8c76187a8f77",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A fan of American author H. P. Lovecraft (1890–1937)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "81e524d9-338b-4ef3-aa3c-d2be6f111692",
                                "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                                "text": "For zealous Lovecraftians there are a few choice tidbits—a short autobiography, his commonplace book, and his “History and Chronology of the Necronomicon.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7b1e2f51-c7ce-49fe-9ab8-d45508818f7c",
                                "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                                "text": "Finally, we can draw a definitive fourth circle, at the absolute heart of HPL’s myth, which contains what most rabid Lovecraftians continue to call, almost in spite of themselves, the “great texts.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8d05a134-2e69-412c-ac26-7337a3f82987",
                                "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                                "text": "Everyone is a “creative anachronist,” but we Lovecraftians, like our cousins in other Buddha-fields of fandom, have elected to live a minority, sectarian existence in what sociologists Berger and Luckmann (The Social Construction of Reality) call a “finite province of meaning.”"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
